                                    <h1 class="static-pagetitle">THE GREAT HISTORY OF THE DOYEN</h1>
                                    <h3>1898: The club is founded</h3>
                                    <h3>1916-1918: Étoile Club Musulman Constantinois (E.C.M.C)</h3>
                                    <p>1917 was the year of the big occasions for the E.C.M.C. That year, the Étoile made its mark with resounding victories:</p>
<p>On 3 June 1917, in a championship match, the E.C.M.C beat U.S. Montpensier in Algiers (2-0). Still in June 1917, the club became North African champion after beating Red Star of Algiers and then Stade Gaulois in Tunis (4-2).</p>
<p>In September 1918, in a tournament organised by the Tunis regional committee and a Tunisian club, the E.C.M.C won big (4-0) against Stade Club Franco-Musulman of Tunis, which was in fact Stade Africain of Tunis.</p>

                                    <h3>1926-1954: Before the Algerian War</h3>
                                    <p>In its first season in the Constantine League, CSC won the second division title (1926-1927).</p>
<p>In the 1930s, the club was strengthened by a merger with the Club Cycliste Musulman Constantinois (C.C.M.C). This led to the creation of several sections and made it a multi-sport club with boxing, volleyball, judo, karate, bowls, athletics and above all its famous swimming and water polo sections, which dominated the department championship in 1939.</p>
<p>In football, CSC qualified for the North African Cup (1933-1934) after beating FC Bougie (Béjaïa) 2-1 in Sétif, then Racing Club Philippevillois (RC Philippeville of Skikda, Constantine League champions in 1932). That match was played in Bône on 26 November 1933 and ended 2-1. CSC then faced SO Sétif in the first round of the North African Cup and won 1-0 in Constantine.</p>
<p>In 1934, the club was knocked out in the round of 16 by Sporting Club de Tunis (in Tunis).</p>
<p>In March 1937, CSC had 4 players in the Constantine League team: striker Lora, goalkeeper Salah, and the two half-backs Bensouiki and Bentorcha, who signed with FC Bordeaux in 1938.</p>
<p>With these players from the Constantine department team, the club won the first division title in 1937-1938.</p>
                                    <h3>Club Populaire de Constantine</h3>
                                    <p>During the Second World War (1939-1945), football activity slowed down and, in 1942, gave way to a competition between neighbourhood clubs, including "Sans-Souci Sportif Constantinois", which represented the old town districts (Houmet Ettabala, Sidi Abdelmoumène, Zelaika, El Batha, Sidi Rached). Later, its officials, players and supporters joined CSC, which first took the name "Club Populaire de Constantine".</p>
<p>In the 1949-50 season, CSC was knocked out of the North African Cup in the round of 32 by Red Star of Algiers.</p>
<p>After the Algerian War broke out in November 1954, the club stopped all activity from 1955 until 1962, the year Algeria won its independence from France.</p>

                                    <h3>1955-1962: During the Algerian War</h3>
                                    <p>CSC gave no fewer than 49 martyrs to Algeria's independence, among its players, athletes and officials. They include the boxer Saïd Bouali, known as "La Motta", and Slimane Mellah, known as "Rachid Mellah". Both activists were part of the historic Group of 22, which met in June 1954 and called for revolution until full independence (the war of independence).</p>
<p>Today, schools and public institutions bear the names of these martyrs who were athletes or officials of the club. Among them is Messaoud Boudjeriou, known as "Messaoud Leksentini", head of Zone 5 of the historic Wilaya II and one of Didouche Mourad's companions, in charge of the resistance in the city of Constantine. Zighoud Youcef made him responsible for the city from July 1955.</p>

                                    <h3>1969-1979: First years in Ligue 1</h3>
                                    <p>After finishing 2nd in the Division d'Honneur in 1969 and winning Nationale II in 1970, CSC earned two promotions in a row and played its first season in Nationale I (the Algerian Ligue 1), where it achieved its first league success: CSC finished runners-up in Algeria in 1970-71. It was the club's only success in Ligue 1 in the 1970s. That same season, CSC took part in one of the longest penalty shoot-outs in football history, in the Algerian Cup round of 16 against Lalmas' CR Belouizdad (49-48), at the Mohamed Guessab Stadium in Sétif.</p>
                                    <h3>1980-1989: The dark years</h3>
                                    <p>The 1980s were the hardest period in the club's history: it played only one season in Ligue 1 (1986-1987). That same season, the club was knocked out in the Algerian Cup semi-finals by JS Bordj Menaïel, 1-0 after extra time, at the Ahmed Zabana Stadium in Oran.</p>
                                    <h3>1990-2000: The first league title</h3>
                                    <p>For the 2nd time in its history, CSC reached the Algerian Cup semi-finals in 1992, losing to ASO Chlef on penalties at the 5 July 1962 Stadium.</p>
<p>Two years later, the club won the Ligue 2 title for the 4th time. That season it dominated D2, including the famous Constantine derby, beating MOC twice (3-0, then 2-0).</p>
<p>Promoted once again in 1994, CSC won the first Algerian league title in its history in 1996-97, with talented and experienced national players (Isaad Bourahli, nicknamed the fox of the box of the 1990s, Mouloud Kaoua, Réda Matem, Salim Laïb, Hassen Ghoula and Sid Ahmed Benamara, international players among others). That same season, CSC reached the final of the Black Stars international tournament in Paris after beating the Senegalese club ASC Diaraf in the semi-final.</p>
<p>The following season (97-98), the club played in the African Champions League for the 1st time. Exempt from the preliminary round, it was knocked out in the first round by the Senegalese club AS Douanes. At the end of the season, CSC finished 2nd in the Algerian championship (Group A).</p>

                                    <h3>2010-2020: Back to glory</h3>
                                    <p>After several years away from the Algerian first division, CSC returned to Ligue 1 after winning the Ligue 2 title in 2011, the 6th in its history.</p>
<p>In the 2011-2012 season, the team narrowly missed the Algerian Cup final (knocked out 1-0 by CR Belouizdad in the semi-final).</p>
<p>After finishing 3rd in the league in 2012-2013 under Roger Lemerre, CS Constantine played in the 2014 Confederation Cup.</p>
<p>In the 2013-14 season, the club played two official matches on the same day (2 February 2014): one in Niger against ASN Nigelec in the CAF Confederation Cup, and the other in Béjaïa against MO Béjaïa on matchday 18 of the league.</p>
<p>The Algerian Football Federation refused to postpone league matches for the Algerian clubs playing in African competitions, because it did not want postponed matches to disrupt a calendar it said was tight because of the World Cup. For this reason, it strongly encouraged the Algerian clubs entered in the 2014 African competitions to withdraw.</p>
<p>Thanks to its fifth place in the league, CSC qualified for the 2016 Confederation Cup, its 2nd appearance. As in 2014, the club was knocked out in the round of 16.</p>
<p>The club kept up fairly steady performances until the 2017-18 season when, 21 years later, it won the second league title in its history under coach Abdelkader Amrani. Thanks to this title, CSC played in the African Champions League for the 2nd time.</p>
<p>On 19 January 2019, the club beat TP Mazembe 3-0 at the Chahid Hamlaoui Stadium. It was the second Algerian win against the Congolese club in the Champions League, and TP Mazembe's heaviest defeat against an Algerian club in that competition.</p>
<p>By beating Club Africain in Tunisia, then TP Mazembe and Ismaily SC in Algeria, CSC secured a historic place in the Champions League quarter-finals, despite losing the last group match against TP Mazembe in Lubumbashi.</p>
